    "title": "The Loop That Follows Your Hand",
    "summary": "Am F C G is i–VI–III–VII, and because each of its four chords has five forms, the loop can be played wherever your hand already is instead of back at the nut.",
    "tags": ["caged", "minor"],
    "readingTimeMin": 4,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-14"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Am F C G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is "
        },
        {
          "text": "i–VI–III–VII",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", one of the most common loops in minor-key popular music. Every one of its four chords comes straight from "
        },
        {
          "text": "the seven the key gives you",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-the-chords-of-the-key"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " — nothing here is borrowed. Play it once before anything else, so what follows is about a sound you've already heard rather than a table you haven't."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "There's no "
        },
        {
          "text": "G#",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " anywhere in this loop. The "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " at the end is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "VII",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and it's exactly the chord that does the pulling in music that never raises its seventh at all."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Here's the loop played straight, in the shapes almost everyone already knows:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
