        {
          "text": "2–6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", lit up with two different chords' worth of dots. The usual convention still holds: the diagram lights every note of the chord inside the window, not the single grip the table above gives — a hand finds a route through it, same as always."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The real point is not which route is better. No one form gives all four of these chords the same window — read down any column of the table above and the four spans never line up — so there is no third route hiding behind the two above. What you already own is five shapes for each of these four chords, twenty places in total, so this loop was never asking you to learn a place on the neck. It is asking which of the twenty you are nearest to right now."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Try it against a "
        },
        {
          "text": "Metronome",
          "link": {
            "kind": "screen",
            "href": "/metronome"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " at a slow comping tempo, changing chord on the bar, and check any of the barred grips against "
        },
        {
          "text": "Chord Shapes",
          "link": {
            "kind": "screen",
            "href": "/chord-shapes"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " for other voicings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "What is left is whether any of this holds up outside A minor — whether the whole loop, forms and all, moves the same way into "
        },
        {
          "text": "a key you have not played it in yet",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
